---
import ReferenceLayout from '../../layouts/ReferenceLayout.astro';

const siteUrl = 'https://bhairavakalikenamosthute.com';
const canonical = `${siteUrl}/about/`;
const title = 'About the Project & Author | Kalabhairava 1000 Names';
const description =
	'About the Kalabhairava 1000 Names project: the author, the Kalabhairava Sahasranama, the source basis, the transliteration approach, and how corrections are handled.';

const schema = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@graph': [
		{
			'@type': 'ProfilePage',
			'@id': `${canonical}#webpage`,
			name: title,
			description,
			url: canonical,
			inLanguage: 'en',
			isPartOf: { '@id': `${siteUrl}/#website` },
			mainEntity: { '@id': `${siteUrl}/#publisher` },
		},
		{
			'@type': 'Person',
			'@id': `${siteUrl}/#publisher`,
			name: 'Kaliputra Ashish',
			description:
				'Author and maintainer of the Kalabhairava 1000 Names project, a free devotional reference for the Kalabhairava Sahasranama.',
			url: canonical,
			sameAs: [
				'https://github.com/09ashishkapoor',
				'https://www.instagram.com/ashishkaliputra',
				'https://archive.org/details/Kalabhairava-sahasranama-kaliputraashish',
			],
			knowsAbout: ['Kalabhairava Sahasranama', 'Bhairava', 'Kashi tradition', 'Shakta and Shaiva devotional literature'],
		},
		{
			'@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
			'@id': `${canonical}#breadcrumb`,
			itemListElement: [
				{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${siteUrl}/` },
				{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'About', item: canonical },
			],
		},
	],
};
---

<ReferenceLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
	<nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
		<ol>
			<li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
			<li><span aria-current="page">About</span></li>
		</ol>
	</nav>

	<p class="page-kicker">About the project</p>
	<h1>About Kalabhairava 1000 Names</h1>

	<figure class="page-figure">
		<img
			src="/kalabhairava-bg-desktop.webp"
			alt="Statue of Kalabhairava holding a trident and sword, with a dog beside him"
			width="1920"
			height="1080"
			loading="lazy"
			decoding="async"
		/>
		<figcaption>Kalabhairava — devotional image used on this site.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<section class="hero-panel">
		<p class="intro">
			<strong>Kalabhairava 1000 Names</strong> is a free devotional reference for the Kalabhairava Sahasranama:
			1,000 names of Sri Kalabhairava with English meanings, Devanagari spellings, and transliteration. It is
			maintained by <strong>Kaliputra Ashish</strong> and offered in the spirit of the Khyapa Parampara.
		</p>
		<nav class="page-nav" aria-label="About navigation">
			<ul>
				<li><a href="/names/">Browse all names</a></li>
				<li><a href="/kalabhairava-sahasranama-pdf/">Free PDF editions</a></li>
				<li><a href="/sources/">Sources and editorial method</a></li>
				<li><a href="/contact/">Contact and corrections</a></li>
			</ul>
		</nav>
	</section>

	<section class="source-notes">
		<h2>What this site is</h2>
		<p>
			The project exists to support devotional reading and study of the Kalabhairava Sahasranama. It presents the
			full 1,000-name text with one-line meanings and longer explanatory notes, a searchable reader, a static names
			hub, individual pages for every name, and printable PDF editions. Everything is offered free, without
			registration.
		</p>
		<p>
			The site is intentionally simple and open: it is a static website with no accounts and no paywall, and its
			source code is public.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="source-notes">
		<h2>Who is Kalabhairava?</h2>
		<p>
			Kalabhairava (Kālabhairava) is a fierce and protective form of Lord Shiva, associated with time (kāla),
			dharma, and the guardianship of sacred space — most famously as the Kotwal of Kashi. For devotees he is both
			terrifying in form and compassionate in nature: he destroys fear, ego and adharma, and protects those who
			take refuge in him.
		</p>
		<p>
			The names in this text are dative salutations (“... namaḥ”), the traditional form for a namavali. Reciting
			them in sequence is a practice of remembrance and surrender.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="source-notes">
		<h2>How the text was compiled</h2>
		<p>
			The names follow the Kalabhairava Sahasranama as compiled for this project. Public reference material and the
			broader Shaiva devotional tradition informed the explanatory notes. The English meanings and elaborations are
			interpretive study notes, not a translation of a single critical edition.
		</p>
		<p>
			The source basis, editorial approach and citation guidance are documented on the
			<a href="/sources/">sources page</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="source-notes">
		<h2>Transliteration approach</h2>
		<p>
			Sanskrit names reach English readers in several romanizations. Each name page shows a common English
			spelling, the IAST form, the Devanagari form and the source transliteration used by this project. The mapping
			is generated from the site's data, so corrections are made once and applied across every page.
		</p>
		<p>
			If a spelling matters for your practice or citation, use the IAST or Devanagari form on the individual name
			page as the reference.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="source-notes">
		<h2>Open source and corrections</h2>
		<p>
			The site is open source. Spelling, meaning, formatting, attribution and source issues can be reported through
			the public repository, which keeps the correction reviewable. See the
			<a href="/contact/">contact page</a> for the best ways to reach the maintainer.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="https://github.com/09ashishkapoor/kalabhairava1000names" rel="noopener noreferrer">View the project repository</a>
		</p>
	</section>
</ReferenceLayout>
